<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      The S3 client behind ObjectStoreAttachmentStorage. One package, not an SDK metapackage: this
      project talks to object storage and nothing else AWS makes. The adapter is written against the
      S3 API rather than against Amazon, because path-style addressing over a configured ServiceUrl
      is what Cloudflare R2, Backblaze B2 and MinIO all speak — so one client serves every store a
      deployment is likely to have.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="AWSSDK.S3" Version="4.0.102.1" />
    <!--
      The SMTP client behind SmtpNotificationSender. MailKit rather than the framework's obsolete
      System.Net.Mail.SmtpClient, which Microsoft marks as not recommended for new development and
      which cannot do STARTTLS negotiation properly. SMTP rather than a provider's HTTP API for the
      reason the adapter gives: every provider a shop might use speaks it, so this repository does
      not have to pick one.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="MailKit" Version="4.17.0" />
    <!--
      The NetTopologySuite plugin rather than the base provider alone: GeoPoint is mapped to
      geography(Point) in step 25, and the plugin is what teaches Npgsql to read and write NTS
      geometries. It brings Npgsql.EntityFrameworkCore.PostgreSQL with it, but the base package
      is named explicitly so the provider version is visible rather than transitive.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Npgsql.EntityFrameworkCore.PostgreSQL" Version="10.0.3" />
    <PackageReference Include="Npgsql.EntityFrameworkCore.PostgreSQL.NetTopologySuite" Version="10.0.3" />
    <!--
      snake_case names, so the schema reads like Postgres rather than like C# in quotation
      marks. Applied as a convention rather than a HasColumnName on every property: the build
      plan's own index in step 27 is jobs(org_id, status, window_start), and three hundred
      hand-written column names is three hundred chances to disagree with it.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="EFCore.NamingConventions" Version="10.0.1" />
    <!--
      MediatR is deliberately absent, against the convention above. `DomainEventDispatcher` uses
      IPublisher, but the package arrives through the Application reference below, where the
      version is pinned to the last Apache-2.0 release and the licensing reason for the pin is
      written out. Naming it here too would put that pin in two places with nothing keeping the
      halves in step — and the half without the comment is the one that would get bumped.
    -->
    <!--
      JwtTokenIssuer writes the tokens Api's Microsoft.AspNetCore.Authentication.JwtBearer
      handler validates. That handler ships in the ASP.NET Core shared framework Api references
      (Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Web") and needs no package here; writing a token is not part of it,
      so Infrastructure — which is plain Microsoft.NET.Sdk and gets no shared framework — needs
      this explicitly.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="System.IdentityModel.Tokens.Jwt" Version="8.15.0" />
    <!--
      Health checks (Document 3, step 54). The endpoints that serve them are ASP.NET Core's and
      live in Api, which gets this through the shared framework; the check itself is a question
      about the database, so it lives beside the context that answers it and needs the package
      explicitly. Nothing here depends on a host: this package is the abstraction and the service,
      not the middleware.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Diagnostics.HealthChecks" Version="10.0.10" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      The integration suite reaches two internals here: OutboxDispatcher.SweepAsync and
      OutboxTrail.ForgetAsync. Both were unreachable from a test, and both held a defect for it —
      the dispatcher swept with no tenant resolved, so every background delivery threw and became a
      poison row, and the trail deleted other tenants' undelivered rows. The half that was extracted
      "so a test can drive a sweep" was the half without the bug. The same arrangement Scheduling
      and Api already have with their own tests.
    -->
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="OpenDispatch.Api.IntegrationTests" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Application\Application.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Domain\Domain.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Scheduling\Scheduling.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
